        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">01 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Platform Overview</h2><p>What you can do across the Hub, and how the sections fit together.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose">
          <p>BNB RWA Hub is a web application for exploring, analyzing and trading tokenized stocks and ETFs on BNB Smart Chain. It brings together assets represented by <strong>bStocks, Ondo and xStocks</strong>, organizing them around their underlying companies or funds rather than treating every provider token as a separate asset.</p>
          <p>The platform combines market information, technical analysis, AI agent research and wallet-based trading. Each section has a distinct purpose, from identifying market activity to investigating an asset and preparing a transaction.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="about-section-label">The main sections</div>
        <div class="about-feature-grid">
          <article class="about-feature"><div class="about-feature-top"><span class="about-feature-glyph">▦</span><span class="about-feature-counter">01</span></div><h3>Dashboard</h3><p>A snapshot of the supported RWA market. Its five tabs cover general market statistics, Market Pulse rankings, technical Opportunities, provider coverage and Data Health.</p><p>Market Pulse includes gainers, losers, underlying trading volume and market-cap rankings, plus <strong>Most Held Stock Tokens</strong> from a saved Binance Wallet Skills snapshot. Opportunity cards show recorded technical evidence, not guaranteed trade recommendations.</p></article>
          <article class="about-feature"><div class="about-feature-top"><span class="about-feature-glyph">▤</span><span class="about-feature-counter">02</span></div><h3>Assets Directory</h3><p>One searchable record per underlying stock or ETF, even if several providers offer related tokens.</p><p>Search by ticker or company name; sort by performance, underlying volume or market capitalization; filter by asset type, provider or technical signal. Assets with missing information remain visible.</p></article>
          <article class="about-feature"><div class="about-feature-top"><span class="about-feature-glyph">◎</span><span class="about-feature-counter">03</span></div><h3>Asset Details</h3><p>Dedicated pages combine asset identity, price and 24-hour performance with underlying market capitalization, volume and rankings.</p><p><strong>RWA representations</strong> keep token symbols, BSC contracts, token prices and token/share ratios separate by provider. A separate Binance Wallet Skills table shows available contract-level holder ranks, KYC/total holder counts, top-10 concentration and independent audit availability.</p></article>
          <article class="about-feature"><div class="about-feature-top"><span class="about-feature-glyph">✳</span><span class="about-feature-counter">04</span></div><h3>Ask Hubble</h3><p>Hubble is the platform's <strong>AI research agent</strong>. It answers natural-language questions about supported RWA assets, compares evidence and can select read-only research tools to investigate available records.</p><p>Its research can include saved Binance Wallet Skills rankings and holder distribution alongside technical indicators. The Ask Hubble page also provides direct navigation and trading controls that can operate independently of the agent.</p></article>
          <article class="about-feature about-feature-wide"><div class="about-feature-top"><span class="about-feature-glyph">⇄</span><span class="about-feature-counter">05</span></div><h3>Buy &amp; Sell</h3><p>Supported trading workflows connect market research to quote-based transactions on BNB Smart Chain. Visitors can begin a purchase from an Asset page or use the trading controls on the Ask Hubble page.</p><p>Quotes, token approvals and swaps remain separate steps. No research answer or open trading dialog transfers funds; approvals and transactions require explicit confirmation through the user's connected wallet.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>How the sections work together</h3><p>An example research journey, not a required sequence.</p></div>
        <div class="about-steps about-steps-compact" aria-label="Example research journey"><div class="about-step"><span>01</span><strong>Discover</strong><small>Dashboard activity and technical candidates</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>02</span><strong>Narrow down</strong><small>Assets search, sorting and filters</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>03</span><strong>Investigate</strong><small>Asset Details and Hubble research</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>04</span><strong>Decide</strong><small>Optional quotes and wallet-controlled trades</small></div></div>
        <div class="about-note"><span class="about-note-icon">i</span><p>Users can explore the market and ask research questions without connecting a wallet. Wallet access becomes relevant only when they choose to interact with supported trading functions.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-panel" id="about-panel-data" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="about-tab-data" tabindex="0">
        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">02 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Data &amp; Providers</h2><p>Where information comes from, what it means, and how we maintain it.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>BNB RWA Hub collects and organizes market information for tokenized stocks and ETFs on BNB Smart Chain. The same underlying asset can have several blockchain representations, so the platform distinguishes the original financial asset from each provider's token.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Data sources</h3></div>
        <div class="about-info-grid"><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">01 / Catalogue</span><h4>Assets &amp; contracts</h4><p>Underlying tickers and names, providers, token symbols, contract addresses and representation metadata.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">02 / Market</span><h4>Prices &amp; activity</h4><p>Available prices, price changes, underlying volume, market capitalization and token-specific data.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">03 / Technical</span><h4>Historical candles</h4><p>Observed daily price series used for indicators and technical signal detection.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>The platform primarily uses <strong>Binance Web3 market and RWA APIs</strong>. Not every field is available for every asset, and similar field names can represent different market concepts. The application processes and stores relevant records in MySQL so that pages and Hubble can reuse consistent information without requesting it all again on each visit.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Binance Wallet Skills data</h3><p>Holder rankings, concentration and supported security information for individual BSC contracts.</p></div>
        <div class="about-info-grid">
          <div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Stock Token Ranking</span><h4>Most held contracts</h4><p>A shared BSC ranking of provider tokens, with holder rank displayed as position / ranked contracts. It is not an underlying-company ranking.</p></div>
          <div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Holder metrics</span><h4>Participation &amp; concentration</h4><p>Binance-reported total holders, KYC holders where supplied, and the share held by the top ten holders, separately for each contract.</p></div>
          <div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Security Audit</span><h4>Availability &amp; limitations</h4><p>Independent contract-audit results when available. Unsupported or unavailable audits display N/A, not a low-risk assessment.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="about-prose">
          <p>BNB RWA Hub collects Binance Wallet Skills <strong>Most Held Stock Tokens</strong> data through a scheduled, read-only background process and saves a validated snapshot. The Dashboard and Hubble read the stored ranking; opening a page or asking a holder question does not start a new Binance request. The collector runs approximately every <strong>30 minutes</strong>, and the interface identifies the snapshot's age.</p>
          <p>In the verified <strong>10 October 2026</strong> snapshot, the ranking covered <strong>488 BSC token contracts</strong>: 46 bStocks and 442 Ondo contracts. These providers share the same ranking denominator. xStocks remained in the broader asset catalogue but was not included in that Binance ranking. The ranked total comes from the collected snapshot and can change; it is not a fixed platform constant.</p>
          <p>Holder numbers are <strong>reported per contract</strong>, not verified unique people and not totals to add across providers. <strong>Holders (KYC / Total)</strong> keeps the two measures distinct; an absent KYC figure means unavailable, not zero. <strong>Top 10 Holder %</strong> indicates reported concentration, not an automatic verdict on token safety or investment risk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="about-note"><span class="about-note-icon">i</span><p>On Asset Detail, the existing <strong>RWA representations</strong> section preserves prices, contract links and token/share ratios. The separate <strong>Token &amp; Security Overview</strong> presents Wallet Skills fields without duplicating those values. A Security Audit result of N/A means unsupported or unavailable; it must not be interpreted as &ldquo;Low risk.&rdquo;</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>One underlying, multiple providers</h3><p>Different tokens can refer to the same company without being interchangeable blockchain assets.</p></div>
        <div class="about-family" aria-label="Example of underlying asset grouping"><div class="about-family-root"><span class="about-family-symbol">NVDA</span><div><strong>Underlying company</strong><small>NVIDIA</small></div></div><div class="about-family-stem" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="about-family-branches"><div><span>bStocks</span><strong>NVDAB</strong></div><div><span>Ondo</span><strong>NVDAon</strong></div><div><span>xStocks</span><strong>NVDAx</strong></div></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Each provider representation has its own contract address and potentially different token characteristics. BNB RWA Hub groups them under the same underlying ticker for discovery, but retains their separate identities on the Asset Detail page.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Why we use this provider order</h3><p>For a single representative underlying record, we prefer <strong>bStocks → Ondo → xStocks</strong>.</p></div>
        <div class="about-priority" aria-label="Provider preference and observed reasons"><div><span class="about-priority-rank">01</span><div><strong>bStocks</strong><p>Our first choice: market data and historical candles were generally the most consistent, up-to-date and usable during development testing.</p></div></div><div><span class="about-priority-rank">02</span><div><strong>Ondo</strong><p>Broad token catalogue coverage, with generally stable and reliable records. It extends coverage when a bStocks representation is unavailable.</p></div></div><div><span class="about-priority-rank">03</span><div><strong>xStocks</strong><p>Included for asset and provider coverage, but placed last because we encountered more frequent outdated price records and incomplete information.</p></div></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>This priority reflects observed data quality and consistency, not investment quality or provider product rankings. Provider values are not averaged or added together. Filtering by provider indicates availability, not a change to the chosen underlying market values.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Underlying data is not token data</h3></div>
        <div class="about-compare-grid"><div class="about-compare-card"><span class="about-compare-tag">Traditional market</span><h4>Underlying asset</h4><p>Company or fund market capitalization, underlying trading volume and reference values.</p></div><div class="about-compare-divider" aria-hidden="true">≠</div><div class="about-compare-card"><span class="about-compare-tag">Blockchain representation</span><h4>Provider token</h4><p>Token contract, token price, its own trading activity and token/share conversion metadata, when available.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>During development, some Tesla-related RWA records reported capitalization and volume at the scale of the underlying US stock market. Treating those numbers as BNB Chain token activity would have been misleading. We therefore label <strong>Underlying Market Cap</strong> and <strong>Underlying 24h Volume</strong> explicitly, rather than presenting them as token market statistics.</p><p>A token/share ratio describes how a provider token relates to an underlying share; it is not itself an arbitrage spread or premium. Provider token prices are shown separately, and recorded token prices are not executable quotes.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Collection &amp; refresh</h3></div>
        <div class="about-pipeline" aria-label="Data processing pipeline"><div><span>Source</span><strong>Binance Web3 / RWA</strong><small>Catalogue, market, candles</small></div><span class="about-pipeline-arrow">→</span><div><span>Collect</span><strong>Scheduled jobs</strong><small>Batched, rate-coordinated</small></div><span class="about-pipeline-arrow">→</span><div><span>Store</span><strong>MySQL snapshots</strong><small>Source + fetch timestamps</small></div><span class="about-pipeline-arrow">→</span><div><span>Read</span><strong>Pages &amp; Hubble</strong><small>Controlled data access</small></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>The catalogue is typically refreshed about daily. General and RWA market collectors run approximately every two minutes; the independent Wallet Skills holder-ranking collector runs approximately every 30 minutes. Completed daily candles are analyzed through a separate technical process, with Ondo coverage rotating in batches. The Dashboard, Assets and Hubble research interfaces reuse saved records rather than calling external market APIs on each page view or research question.</p><p>Trading quotes follow a separate path because real execution requires current transaction-specific route information rather than stored snapshots.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Freshness is more than one timestamp</h3></div>
        <div class="about-info-grid"><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Source time</span><h4>When the value changed</h4><p>The original price or market value's reported update time, if available.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Fetch time</span><h4>When we retrieved it</h4><p>A successful new API fetch does not automatically make an old source price current.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Candle time</span><h4>When evidence closed</h4><p>The completed price candle underlying a technical observation, tracked separately from the technical fetch time.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Asset Details show the relevant timestamps, Dashboard Data Health summarizes collection and technical coverage, and Wallet Skills panels identify the ranking snapshot age. A recent collector run does not imply every original market observation has changed. Missing data remains unavailable rather than becoming a fabricated zero. Fields with ambiguous meanings are not combined into invented total token volumes, liquidity or provider market shares.</p></div>
        <div class="about-note"><span class="about-note-icon">i</span><p>Catalogue coverage does not guarantee complete price histories or available trading routes. A saved price helps research; it does not guarantee an executable transaction at that price.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-panel" id="about-panel-technical" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="about-tab-technical" tabindex="0">
        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">03 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Technical Analysis</h2><p>Recorded daily candles, reproducible indicators and five rule-based signals.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>BNB RWA Hub processes historical market data through a separate technical scanner, rather than calculating indicators every time a visitor opens a page. The scanner analyzes <strong>observed, completed daily token-price candles</strong>, calculates indicators and publishes a saved technical snapshot for the application.</p><p>These calculations are deterministic and do not depend on Hubble. The AI agent can subsequently examine the evidence and prepare an interpretation.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Indicators</h3></div>
        <div class="about-indicator-grid"><div><strong>SMA20</strong><span>20 observed closes</span><p>Shorter-term moving average.</p></div><div><strong>SMA50</strong><span>50 observed closes</span><p>Broader trend reference.</p></div><div><strong>SMA200</strong><span>200 observed closes</span><p>Longer-term context, if available.</p></div><div><strong>RSI14</strong><span>14 periods</span><p>Price-momentum indicator.</p></div><div><strong>Support / Resistance</strong><span>Repeated turning points</span><p>Observed price zones.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Indicators use actual observed, time-ordered candles. Missing calendar dates are not filled with synthetic prices. If sufficient history is unavailable, the affected indicator remains unavailable rather than being estimated.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Five technical signals</h3><p>Defined screening conditions, not model-invented labels.</p></div>
        <div class="about-signal-list">
          <article class="about-signal"><span class="about-signal-id">01</span><div><h4>Trend Alignment</h4><p>Finds an upward moving-average structure with moderate momentum. The latest close is above SMA20, which is above SMA50; RSI14 is between 45 and 70.</p><div class="about-signal-rule">Close &gt; SMA20 &gt; SMA50 <span>·</span> RSI14 45–70</div></div></article>
          <article class="about-signal"><span class="about-signal-id">02</span><div><h4>MA Pullback Recovery</h4><p>Looks for a possible recovery near the shorter moving average while the broader uptrend remains intact. Close is at or above SMA50 and within 1.5% of SMA20; RSI14 is between 40 and 65 and rising.</p><div class="about-signal-rule">SMA20 &gt; SMA50 <span>·</span> Near SMA20 <span>·</span> RSI improving</div></div></article>
          <article class="about-signal"><span class="about-signal-id">03</span><div><h4>Support Recovery</h4><p>Examines a repeated support pivot within 2% of the relevant price, along with a rising close and RSI14 between 40 and 65. It identifies a possible response near support, not proof that support will hold.</p><div class="about-signal-rule">Repeated support <span>·</span> Within 2% <span>·</span> Rising close</div></div></article>
          <article class="about-signal"><span class="about-signal-id">04</span><div><h4>Possible Range Breakout</h4><p>Checks whether the latest close exceeds an earlier 20-candle range high by more than 0.3%, without the previous close already being above that threshold. RSI14 is between 50 and 75.</p><div class="about-signal-rule">Prior range high +0.3% <span>·</span> New close above <span>·</span> RSI14 50–75</div></div></article>
          <article class="about-signal"><span class="about-signal-id">05</span><div><h4>Possible Breakout Retest</h4><p>Looks for a return toward a previously broken resistance level. The latest candle approaches within 0.5% of that level and closes above it, with RSI14 between 45 and 75.</p><div class="about-signal-rule">Previous breakout <span>·</span> Retest within 0.5% <span>·</span> Close above</div></div></article>
        </div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>From signals to research</h3></div>
        <div class="about-steps about-steps-compact"><div class="about-step"><span>01</span><strong>Read candles</strong><small>Completed provider-token series</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>02</span><strong>Calculate</strong><small>SMA, RSI, repeated zones</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>03</span><strong>Screen</strong><small>Predefined pattern rules</small></div><div class="about-step"><span>04</span><strong>Interpret</strong><small>Dashboard, Asset pages, Hubble</small></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>One asset can match several signals. The Dashboard displays candidates with technical evidence; Assets supports signal filters; Asset Details shows individual indicators; and Hubble may examine and compare candidates when a user asks for research.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Coverage &amp; quality controls</h3></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>bStocks is the primary technical source, with Ondo extending coverage where a bStocks representation is absent. xStocks-only assets are currently excluded from technical opportunity scanning. The system does not combine providers' candles into synthetic history.</p><p>Unfinished candles, invalid values, conflicting observations, insufficient histories and suspicious scale changes are handled through validation rules. Data that is too old may remain available as recorded evidence but cannot establish a current opportunity. For current candidate generation, the completed daily candle must satisfy the platform's 24-hour freshness rule.</p><p>These calculations currently describe <strong>provider-token price histories</strong>, not independently normalized stock-price histories. Corporate actions, historical token/share conversions, executable prices, fees and slippage are not established by the technical scanner.</p></div>
        <div class="about-note about-note-amber"><span class="about-note-icon">!</span><p>A signal is an observable condition, not a probability of success, a backtested prediction or a trading instruction. Hubble evaluates technical evidence; it does not change the underlying indicator calculations.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-panel" id="about-panel-hubble" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="about-tab-hubble" tabindex="0">
        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">04 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Ask Hubble</h2><p>The AI research agent, its tools, and the boundaries between agent decisions and application logic.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose"><p><strong>Hubble is BNB RWA Hub's AI research agent.</strong> Users interact with it through the Ask Hubble page, which also contains direct navigation and trading controls that can operate independently of the agent.</p><p>Hubble investigates supported tokenized stocks and ETFs using natural-language questions. Instead of relying solely on general model knowledge, it can use the platform's controlled research tools to examine stored market, provider and technical records.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>How a research request works</h3></div>
        <div class="about-research-flow" aria-label="Hubble research process"><div><span>01</span><h4>Interpret</h4><p>Understand the question, assets and objectives.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h4>Choose</h4><p>Select relevant read-only research tools.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h4>Retrieve</h4><p>Obtain permitted saved data from the Hub.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h4>Evaluate</h4><p>Compare evidence, gaps and freshness.</p></div><div><span>05</span><h4>Explain</h4><p>Answer in prose, summaries or tables.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Hubble can choose research operations according to the question. For example, a comparison of several companies requires different records from a provider-specific ETF ranking or a data-freshness investigation. The application controls the permitted operations, input validation and database access. Hubble has no unrestricted database credentials or permission to modify financial data.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Holder intelligence in Hubble research</h3><p>Use the same saved Binance Wallet Skills evidence as the Dashboard and Asset Detail pages.</p></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Hubble can answer direct questions about the <strong>most held stock tokens</strong>, compare holder and KYC counts between provider contracts, and add holder concentration and rank information to a supported asset's technical analysis. A straightforward most-held list can be returned as a database-backed table; comparative explanations and mixed technical/holder analysis can be written from validated evidence.</p><p>Provider-contract rankings are not presented as underlying-level holder totals. A missing entry for a provider is reported as unavailable, not zero. Holder concentration is one research metric, not a security-audit result, and technical indicators retain their own source-candle and freshness rules.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>What the agent decides — and what the software controls</h3></div>
        <div class="about-dual"><div class="about-dual-ai"><span class="about-dual-header">Hubble / AI agent</span><ul><li>Interprets research questions</li><li>Selects appropriate permitted research tools</li><li>Compares supplied evidence</li><li>Ranks and explains eligible technical candidates</li><li>Organizes responses into readable text and tables</li></ul></div><div class="about-dual-system"><span class="about-dual-header">BNB RWA Hub / Software</span><ul><li>Collects prices and provider information</li><li>Controls database access and validation</li><li>Calculates indicators and technical signals</li><li>Tracks source freshness and missing values</li><li>Controls trading and wallet safety checks</li></ul></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Hubble does not calculate market prices or independently manufacture indicator values. For example, when it compares NVDA, AMD and MSFT, the market capitalization figures come from the Hub's stored data. The agent chooses how to compare and explain them.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Opportunity discovery</h3></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>The technical engine first identifies assets matching predefined conditions. Hubble receives the eligible candidate evidence and evaluates it to prepare its research assessment. The deterministic prefilter and any internal software points are not presented as the agent's own ranking.</p><p>Users can request a specific number of results within the supported batch range and continue with <strong>Show more</strong>. The agent retains the active ranking state and position across batches, while model request windows and cooldowns may temporarily limit further requests.</p><p>Technical candidate information has its own freshness requirements. A pattern found in stale price history may be discussed as recorded evidence, but should not be presented as a confirmed current opportunity.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Direct actions are different from agent research</h3></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Clearly recognized actions and direct interface controls can operate without an AI decision. A direct request such as <code>Buy NVDA</code> can open the existing purchase flow, while an informational question such as <code>Where can I buy NVDA?</code> should receive an answer rather than unexpectedly opening a transaction dialog.</p><p>Direct Buy and Sell buttons on the Ask Hubble page use the platform's trading components. Opening a trading interface does not authorize an approval or transaction. Conversation clearing is also a direct user-interface action, not a destructive instruction delegated to the model.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Example questions</h3><p>Different questions exercise different research tools and response types.</p></div>
        <div class="about-example-grid">
          <div><span>Individual analysis</span><code>Analyze TSLA.</code><p>Review available market and technical evidence.</p></div>
          <div><span>Multiple assets</span><code>Compare NVDA, AMD and MSFT by market cap and 24h performance.</code><p>Compare saved underlying values.</p></div>
          <div><span>Provider research</span><code>Show me the 5 biggest ETFs available on Ondo.</code><p>Filter the catalogue and underlying metrics.</p></div>
          <div><span>Opportunity research</span><code>Find 5 RWA opportunities.</code><p>Evaluate eligible technical candidates.</p></div>
          <div><span>Representations</span><code>Compare TSLA providers.</code><p>Examine available token representations.</p></div>
          <div><span>Data quality</span><code>Which assets have outdated market data?</code><p>Investigate recorded timestamps and availability.</p></div>
          <div><span>Wallet Skills ranking</span><code>What are the 5 most held stock tokens on BNB Chain?</code><p>Read the saved contract ranking and reported holder counts.</p></div>
          <div><span>Holder comparison</span><code>Compare NVDAB and NVDAon by holders, KYC holders and concentration.</code><p>Compare separate provider contracts without combining their holders.</p></div>
          <div><span>Combined research</span><code>Analyze NVDA in detail, including holder distribution and technical indicators.</code><p>Combine dated technical evidence with contract-level participation metrics.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Model infrastructure &amp; conversation history</h3></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Hubble uses a language model through the <strong>Groq inference service</strong>. The model provides reasoning and natural-language output; the Hub provides read-only research tools, financial records and application rules. Model services have request and output limits, so the integration includes cooldown handling and reports model unavailability rather than silently inventing a response.</p><p>Conversation history is stored by the platform so users can continue a research session without account registration. The browser session can restore earlier interactions for a limited period, and users can clear their conversation history through the <strong>Clear</strong> control.</p></div>
        <div class="about-note"><span class="about-note-icon">i</span><p>Hubble can investigate and explain; it cannot approve token spending or submit wallet transactions on the user's behalf. Its conclusions depend on available evidence and are not guaranteed market forecasts.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-panel" id="about-panel-trading" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="about-tab-trading" tabindex="0">
        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">05 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Trading &amp; Security</h2><p>Quote-based buy and sell operations, transaction review and wallet authorization.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>BNB RWA Hub supports wallet-based trading for eligible tokenized stocks and ETFs on BNB Smart Chain. Trading is separate from research: visitors can browse market data and ask Hubble questions without connecting a wallet.</p><p>The platform coordinates the interface, input checks and transaction preparation. Binance Web3 trading APIs and supported LiquidMesh swap routes provide available quotes and route information; BNB Smart Chain processes submitted transactions. The <strong>user's connected wallet</strong> provides authorization and signatures.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Recorded market prices vs. executable quotes</h3></div>
        <div class="about-compare-grid"><div class="about-compare-card"><span class="about-compare-tag">Research display</span><h4>Saved market price</h4><p>Collected earlier for the Dashboard, Assets and Asset Details. Useful context, but not a guaranteed trade price.</p></div><div class="about-compare-divider" aria-hidden="true">≠</div><div class="about-compare-card"><span class="about-compare-tag">Trade preparation</span><h4>Current route quote</h4><p>Requested for a particular direction and amount, with expected proceeds and route requirements. May change or expire.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>Execution conditions, fees, slippage, market movement and route availability can make a real quote differ from the displayed market price. Not every listed asset or provider token has a usable execution route.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Buying RWA tokens</h3><p>Available from an Asset Detail page or the direct controls on Ask Hubble.</p></div>
        <div class="about-trade-timeline"><div><span>01</span><div><h4>Connect</h4><p>Choose a compatible wallet and use BNB Smart Chain.</p></div></div><div><span>02</span><div><h4>Set amount</h4><p>Specify how much USDT to spend. The current demo allows purchases of 6–10 USDT.</p></div></div><div><span>03</span><div><h4>Compare quotes</h4><p>Review available provider/route alternatives and expected token amounts.</p></div></div><div><span>04</span><div><h4>Review &amp; approve</h4><p>Examine transaction details and authorize any required token spending permission separately.</p></div></div><div><span>05</span><div><h4>Confirm purchase</h4><p>Explicitly sign the purchase transaction in the user's connected wallet.</p></div></div></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Selling RWA tokens</h3></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>For sales, the platform can check eligible RWA token balances in the connected wallet through read-only blockchain requests. The user chooses a supported token and amount, obtains a fresh quote for expected USDT proceeds and reviews transaction requirements.</p><p>The current demo limits estimated sale proceeds to <strong>20 USDT</strong> and supports eligible sale tokens with 18 decimal places. Token balance, allowance, chain, contract and route checks remain part of the flow. Submitted transaction status can be checked, and a prepared quote alone is never reported as a successful sale.</p><p>The Swap navigation entry offers access to the supported buy and sell workflows; it does not imply that every arbitrary token pair can be exchanged.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Why approval and swap are separate</h3></div>
        <div class="about-dual"><div><span class="about-dual-header">Token approval</span><p>A wallet-authorized permission for a specified contract to spend the selected amount of a token. Approval does not itself execute a purchase or sale.</p></div><div><span class="about-dual-header">Swap transaction</span><p>The separate on-chain operation that attempts the actual exchange through a supported route. It requires its own wallet confirmation.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>BNB RWA Hub uses amount-specific permissions rather than automatically asking for unrestricted token allowances. The user's connected wallet must explicitly review and confirm any required approval and transaction. BNB network gas is additional.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Validation &amp; user control</h3></div>
        <div class="about-check-grid"><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Network, token and contract parameters</div><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Supported amount and precision</div><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Quote freshness and route consistency</div><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Allowance and spender verification</div><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Transaction preparation and review</div><div><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>Submission status and result checks</div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>These controls reduce the risk of unintended permissions, incorrect routing and misleading transaction status. They do not eliminate smart contract, price movement, gas, network or wallet risks. Hubble cannot override the validation process or sign a transaction.</p></div>
        <div class="about-note about-note-amber"><span class="about-note-icon">!</span><p>This is a controlled hackathon implementation, not an unrestricted brokerage or exchange. Demo amount limits and route requirements apply. A valid quote does not guarantee on-chain execution or profitability.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-panel" id="about-panel-engineering" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="about-tab-engineering" tabindex="0">
        <header class="about-panel-head"><div class="about-panel-index">06 <span>/ 06</span></div><div><h2>Engineering Notes</h2><p>Practical implementation choices and problems encountered during development.</p></div></header>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>BNB RWA Hub combines financial market APIs, historical analysis, AI research and live blockchain operations. These systems have different reliability, freshness and security requirements. The architecture separates them so failures in one component do not unnecessarily become failures everywhere else.</p></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Architecture at a glance</h3></div>
        <div class="about-stack-grid"><div><span>Web application</span><strong>PHP + JavaScript</strong><p>Interface, validation and endpoints</p></div><div><span>Data storage</span><strong>MySQL</strong><p>Catalogue, market and research records</p></div><div><span>Technical engine</span><strong>Python</strong><p>Candle validation and rule-based scans</p></div><div><span>Agent service</span><strong>TypeScript + Groq</strong><p>Controlled AI research and responses</p></div><div><span>Operations</span><strong>Linux + systemd</strong><p>Collectors, scheduling and service status</p></div><div><span>Execution</span><strong>BNB Smart Chain</strong><p>Quotes, wallet and transaction verification</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Problems we encountered — and how we addressed them</h3></div>
        <div class="about-engineering-list">
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">01</div><div><h4>Inconsistent financial data semantics</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Similar-looking API fields mixed underlying US equity statistics with token-specific information, leading to potentially misleading volume and market-cap comparisons.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Keep provider-token records distinct from underlying market records, persist one selected underlying snapshot per asset, and leave ambiguous values out of aggregate metrics. Missing data remains missing.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">02</div><div><h4>Large catalogues and request limits</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Refreshing hundreds of token records can consume API capacity, and oversized collector batches can fail or take too long.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Use scheduled background collectors, bounded batches and request-weight/time logging where available. We corrected a collector configuration-path issue and reduced its batch size; subsequent full refresh and scheduled execution were verified.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">03</div><div><h4>Background synchronization delaying quotes</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> An early shared lock could be held throughout a long-running synchronization job, blocking interactive quote requests.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Hold the shared API coordinator only around individual request attempts, while keeping separate job-level locks to prevent duplicate background jobs. This allows request windows to be shared more fairly.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">04</div><div><h4>Incomplete and irregular price history</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Missing trading dates, insufficient historical candles and anomalous price scales could distort moving averages or momentum signals.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Use actual closed observations without fabricating gaps, validate candle fields and preserve diagnostic context. Suspected scale discontinuities are not silently replaced with guessed prices, and stale candles cannot qualify as current opportunities.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">05</div><div><h4>Repeated calculations across pages</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Dashboard, Assets, Asset Details and Hubble all need related information, but independently recalculating it could produce inconsistencies and repeated API traffic.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Save canonical underlying rankings and technical snapshots in MySQL, then expose controlled read-only projections. Market data collection and technical processing run independently of ordinary page requests.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">06</div><div><h4>AI flexibility without unrestricted access</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> The agent needs freedom to decide how to research a question, but must not invent financial values or gain unrestricted database or wallet privileges.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Combine deterministic application actions with agent-selected, bounded read-only research tools. Software owns financial calculations, input validation and transaction checks; Hubble interprets and explains the evidence.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">07</div><div><h4>Model limits and continuity across requests</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Inference providers may return rate limits or incomplete responses, while opportunity research needs to continue across multiple result batches.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Use cooldown windows, explicit unavailable/error states, stored conversation and ranking position, and model-backed pagination. Failed AI ranking is never disguised as a software-generated agent judgment.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">08</div><div><h4>Real wallet operations and uncertain outcomes</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> A displayed quote, wallet request or transaction submission is not proof that a purchase or sale succeeded.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Validate route parameters, separate approval from swap, require the user's wallet to authorize each transaction, and verify reported results. Uncertain outcomes are not automatically treated as success or resubmitted.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">09</div><div><h4>Refresh behavior and meaningful error states</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> A new API fetch can still contain old source prices; temporary service failures should not make already loaded pages appear empty.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Track source, fetch and candle times separately. Use quiet page refreshes, pause unnecessary background-tab polling, preserve visible records on transient failures and distinguish missing data, stale values and pending transactions.</p></div></article>
          <article class="about-engineering-item"><div class="about-engineering-id">10</div><div><h4>Contract-accurate Wallet Skills evidence</h4><p><strong>Challenge.</strong> Stock-token ranking rows can span multiple providers and may omit KYC counts or unsupported audit results. Treating provider rankings as underlying totals, converting missing values to zero or assigning a risk grade to an unsupported audit would mislead users.</p><p><strong>Approach.</strong> Match rank records to BSC token contracts, retain a shared snapshot denominator and contract-specific holder metrics, and distinguish missing KYC data from actual zero. Keep audit availability independent of concentration and present N/A when no supported audit exists. Serve validated data to Dashboard, Asset Detail and Hubble through read-only paths.</p></div></article>
        </div>
        <div class="about-section-heading"><h3>Verification is not a single test</h3></div>
        <div class="about-info-grid"><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Local</span><h4>Syntax &amp; mocked logic</h4><p>Verifies application rules and expected failure behavior without proving live deployment.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">VPS</span><h4>Services &amp; external APIs</h4><p>Checks actual scheduled jobs, database updates, requests and deployed behavior.</p></div><div class="about-info-card"><span class="about-info-label">Wallet</span><h4>Real transaction outcomes</h4><p>Separately establishes on-chain submission and verified state changes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="about-prose"><p>The current implementation deliberately retains scope limitations. Not every provider offers complete data, technical signals are not validated as predictive investment strategies, and execution depends on supported token and route conditions. These constraints are identified rather than hidden behind an artificially complete presentation.</p></div>
        <div class="about-note"><span class="about-note-icon">i</span><p>The engineering priority is to preserve data meaning, keep automated research within controlled boundaries, and require explicit user authorization for financial transactions.</p></div>
      </section>
